CreditScoreService: score=750
IncomeVerificationService: verified=true
PropertyValuationService: value=7500000.00
LegalTitleService: clear=true
DisbursementService: disbursed 5000000.00
Result: APPROVED
```

### How is the Facade different from Adapter, Mediator, and Proxy?

| Pattern | Purpose | Direction |
|---|---|---|
| **Facade** | Simplifies a complex subsystem into one interface | One-way; subsystem does not know the facade |
| **Adapter** | Converts one interface to another so incompatible things fit | Wraps one object |
| **Mediator** | Coordinates two-way interaction between peers | Bidirectional; peers talk back to mediator |
| **Proxy** | Stands in for one object to control access, caching, laziness | Wraps one object, same interface |

### Why must the facade stay thin?

If the facade starts holding real business rules it becomes a God object violating single responsibility. The real logic stays in the subsystem classes; the facade only orchestrates.

```java
// WRONG: facade holds business logic
public LoanDecision approve(String customerId, BigDecimal amount) {
    // calculating EMI, applying interest rates, checking blacklists HERE
    // turns the facade into a God object
}

// CORRECT: facade delegates to specialists
public LoanDecision approve(String customerId, BigDecimal amount) {
    if (creditScore.fetch(customerId) < 700) return rejected();
    // each specialist holds its own logic
}
```

### What are typical use cases?

- A `@Service` class in Spring that orchestrates repositories, validators, and external clients to fulfil one business operation.
- Wrapping a complicated third-party library so the rest of the code calls a simple in-house interface.
- One `onboardCustomer` or `processLoan` method that coordinates many steps.
- An API Gateway presenting one endpoint that fans out to many internal services.

### How does a facade relate to microservices?

At the network level, an API Gateway is essentially a facade: it presents one entry point, coordinates calls to multiple backend services, and hides the internal topology from clients.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The facade is an excellent place to apply a single transaction boundary, consistent logging, and uniform error translation for a whole workflow.
- You can have several facades over the same subsystem for different client needs.
- The pattern shines when onboarding new developers, who can accomplish common tasks through the facade without learning the entire subsystem.
- In Spring, constructor injection makes the delegation explicit and testable.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can a facade call another facade? (Yes, but keep the depth shallow to avoid hidden complexity.)
- How do you test a facade? (Unit test with mocked subsystem classes; integration test the full chain.)
- When should you NOT use a facade? (When there is no subsystem complexity to hide; adding a facade around one class is pointless indirection.)
